As  a  final  point,  we  should  note  that  according  to  the  modern  understanding 
of  the  precession  of  the  equinoxes,  the  present  polestar,  Polaris,  is  now  about  1 0 
from  the  north  celestial  pole  and  will  pass  within  28'  of  it  around  the  year  2100. 
One  thousand  years  ago,  Polaris  was  about  6.5°  away  from  the  pole,  and  a 
thousand  years  before  that  it  was  about  12°  away.  Since  6.5°  is  about  13  limes 
the  width  of  the  full  moon,  it  is  hard  to  see  how  Polaris  could  have  been  regarded 
as  the  polestar  prior  to  one  thousand  years  ago. 

Due  to  a  lack  of  suitable  bright  stars,  it  would  appear  that  there  was  no 
prominent  polestar  from  a  few  centuries  ago  back  to  about  1200  B.C.  At  about 
